From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Send the frame rate that was agreed on

A receiver is told during setup how large a picture it will be sent and how
often, and it expects to be sent that. Nothing held the capture to the rate
in question. The filters either side of the scaler name a rate in their caps,
but caps settle what a pad may carry rather than how often a frame arrives
over it, and the one on the capture side names a ceiling rather than a rate
at all. No element in the chain measured the rate or held it to anything.

A desktop that produced frames faster than the rate agreed on therefore sent
every one of them. Counted over ten seconds, a link set up for 30 frames a
second was being sent about 36. The receiver threw the surplus away, which is
seen as the picture catching about once a second while the sender reports
nothing wrong, because from the sender's side nothing was.

The surplus cost twice over. The receiver discarded a fifth of what it was
sent, and the rate the picture is held to was divided over the larger number,
so every frame was given about a sixth fewer bits than the rate control had
set aside for it.

The rate is now held to what was agreed, before anything is scaled or
encoded, so no work is spent on frames that were never going to be shown.
Frames are only dropped, never invented, so a desktop with nothing moving on
it still sends nothing, and the agreed rate stays a ceiling rather than
becoming a quota to fill.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
@@ -187,6 +187,7 @@
 
   QOSData *qos_data;
 
+  GstElement *videorate;
   GstElement *scale;
   GstElement *sinkfilter;
   GstElement *convert;
@@ -205,6 +206,23 @@
   g_assert (source);
   success &= gst_bin_add (bin, source);
 
+  /* A receiver is told a rate during setup and expects to be sent that rate.
+   * Nothing held the capture to it: the filters name a rate in their caps,
+   * but caps settle what a pad may carry and do not govern how often a frame
+   * arrives, and the one on the capture side names a ceiling rather than a
+   * rate at all. A desktop that produced frames faster than the rate agreed
+   * on sent every one of them, and the receiver threw the surplus away. The
+   * rate is held here instead, before anything is scaled or encoded, so the
+   * work is not done on frames that were never going to be shown. Frames are
+   * only ever dropped, never invented: a desktop with nothing moving on it
+   * still sends nothing. */
+  videorate = gst_element_factory_make ("videorate", "wfd-videorate");
+  g_object_set (videorate,
+                "drop-only", TRUE,
+                "skip-to-first", TRUE,
+                NULL);
+  success &= gst_bin_add (bin, videorate);
+
   scale = gst_element_factory_make ("videoscale", "wfd-scale");
   g_object_set (scale,
                 "qos", TRUE,
@@ -384,6 +402,7 @@
                 NULL);
 
   success &= gst_element_link_many (source,
+                                    videorate,
                                     scale,
                                     sinkfilter,
                                     convert,
@@ -617,6 +636,7 @@
 wfd_configure_media_element (GstBin *bin, WfdParams *params)
 {
   g_autoptr(GstCaps) caps = NULL;
+  g_autoptr(GstElement) videorate = NULL;
   g_autoptr(GstElement) srcfilter = NULL;
   g_autoptr(GstElement) sinkfilter = NULL;
   g_autoptr(GstElement) codecfilter = NULL;
@@ -674,6 +694,17 @@
   if (params->idr_request_capability && !(quirks & WFD_QUIRK_NO_IDR))
     gop_size = 10 * resolution->refresh_rate;
 
+  /* The rate agreed with this receiver, now that it is known. */
+  videorate = gst_bin_get_by_name (bin, "wfd-videorate");
+  if (videorate != NULL)
+    {
+      g_object_set (videorate,
+                    "max-rate", (gint) resolution->refresh_rate,
+                    NULL);
+      g_message ("WfdMediaFactory: Sending at most %u frames a second, as agreed.",
+                 resolution->refresh_rate);
+    }
+
   srcfilter = gst_bin_get_by_name (bin, "srcfilter");
   if (srcfilter != NULL)
     {
